\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How WhatsApp OTP actually works\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A user triggers authentication in your app, your backend generates a random 4-6 digit code with a defined expiry, and that code is sent via the WhatsApp Business API using a pre-approved message template \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Meta calls this an 
\f0\b authentication template
\f1\b0 , and every message text has to be submitted and approved in advance, similar in spirit to DLT template approval but through Meta rather than TRAI. \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The user receives it instantly on WhatsApp from your verified business account, enters it in your app, and your backend validates it against the generated value and expiry. \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Newer implementations add a "tap to autofill" button so the code drops straight into the app without the user manually retyping it, and Meta's Zero-Tap feature (Android first) can skip manual entry entirely
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Access path:
\f1\b0  \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You access WhatsApp's Business API either directly or, more commonly for a startup, through a Business Solution Provider (BSP) \'97 Interakt, Gupshup, Wati, Twilio, etc. \
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Onboarding involves Meta business verification, not TRAI/DLT \'97 different regulatory track entirely, which is why it sidesteps your incorporation blocker\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Where WhatsApp OTP 
\f2\i \strokec2 does
\f1\i0 \strokec2  raise the bar slightly:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Registering a WhatsApp account itself has friction
\f1\b0  \'97 it typically requires an active, working SIM capable of receiving SMS/calls, and WhatsApp has its own anti-abuse systems that make it harder (though not impossible) to mass-register accounts on virtual/VoIP numbers compared to raw SMS OTP APIs, which are often abused precisely because burner-number SMS reception is cheap and automatable.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 India-specific tailwind:
\f1\b0  new SIM cards require Aadhaar-linked KYC at the telecom level, so a working Indian number tied to a WhatsApp account already carries some real-world identity binding upstream \'97 that's a property of the Indian telecom system, not of WhatsApp or SMS specifically, and it applies whichever OTP channel you pick.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

